#{[17592186115073 :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n$$\nE^{-1} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{2}}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [17592186115074 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\nE_2^{-1} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nE_1 E_{2}^{-1} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ - 5 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ - 5 & - 9\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$"] [17592186115735 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms 