$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186265975 :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 10 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186265980 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186265982 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ 